drvPExpert - i386 platform expert
=================================

The i386 counterpart of drivers-ppc/bus/drvPExpert, built the same way: an
Aggregate whose Kernel Server tool, i386, produces one relocatable,
pexperti386.o, installed in /usr/local/lib and linked into mach_kernel
through LIBPEXPERT (kernel-7/conf/MASTER.i386, kernel-7/conf/Makefile.i386).
Where the ppc expert carries the motherboard chips and families, this one
carries the i386 buses: the EISA / ISA Plug and Play bus (from drvEISABus),
the PCI bus (from drvPCIBus) and the PCMCIA bus (from drvPCMCIABus), so that
the kernel no longer needs the three loadable bus bundles.  The chips/ and
families/ half described in
docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-24-i386-platform-expert-design.md lands
beside them in the same Kernel Server when it comes.

Layout
------

    drvPExpert/
      apk/pkginfo             drvpexpert, a kernel core package (rbuild kernel)
      i386/                   Kernel Server -> pexperti386.o
        PExpert.h, PExpert.m  the "Bus Class" and the settings lookup
        eisa/                 EISAKernBus, EISAResourceDriver, PnP  (Component)
        pci/                  PCIKernBus, PCIResourceDriver        (Component)
        pcmcia/               PCMCIAKernBus, PCMCIAResourceDriver  (Component)
      PnPDump.tproj/          the PnP dump tool EISABus.config shipped

The bus sources are copies of the three original drivers with only the
changes the merge needed (below).  The originals stay in the tree until the
kernel-linked buses have been boot-tested; then they can go.  Do not install
their bundles alongside a kernel that carries this object: a loaded
EISABus_reloc would register a second EISAKernBus class.

How it comes up
---------------

Linked in, the expert has no bundle for the booter to load, so its config
table is linked in as well: autoconf_i386.m serves inKernelConfigTables
after the booter's tables, the way autoconf_ppc.m does for PPCKernBus.
That table names PExpert as its "Bus Class".  The kernel sends probeBus: to
it before configuring any driver, and PExpert brings the buses up in a
fixed order - EISA first, because it is the kernel's default bus and PCI
delegates resource allocation to it, then PCI, then PCMCIA - and claims the
motherboard's own ports and the 8259 cascade IRQ on the EISA bus.  The
kernel then probes each class in the table's "Class Names", which registers
the EISA0, PCI0 and PCMCIA0 resource devices that Configure.app and
driverLoader use to auto-detect cards.

Two things moved out of the original drivers:

 * The motherboard resources.  Apple's EISABus table reserved
   "I/O Ports" = "0x00-0x0f 0x20-0x21 0x40-0x4b 0x70-0x71 0x81-0x8F 0x92-0x92
   0xc0-0xcf" and "IRQ Levels" = "2" through its EISA0 device.  With one
   table shared by three resource drivers the second driver's allocation
   would fail and the kernel would free a table the first still holds, so
   PExpert reserves them in code (reserveMotherboardResources) and the table
   carries no resource keys.
 * The per-bus settings.  Each driver used to look up its own bundle's
   table by "Server Name".  Both copies of that walk are replaced by
   PExpertAttribute(), which reads the linked-in table, so these keys are
   set in inKernelConfigTables in autoconf_i386.m and take a kernel rebuild:

       "PnP"                  = "No"     disables ISA Plug and Play
       "PnP Read Port"        = "0x203"  fixes the PnP read port
       "Verbose"              = "Yes"    PCMCIA bus logging
       "PCMCIA Memory Base"   = "0xD0000"
       "PCMCIA Memory Length" = "0x2000"

Adding a bus
------------

 1. Create a Component subproject beside eisa/, pci/ and pcmcia/ (copy
    pci/'s Makefile, Makefile.preamble and PB.project) holding a KernBus
    subclass whose +initialize registers it (registerBusClass:name:) and an
    IODirectDevice resource driver that registers "<Bus>0".
 2. Add it to SUBPROJECTS in i386/Makefile and i386/PB.project.
 3. Add the resource driver to the "Class Names" of the PExpert table in
    kernel-7/driverkit/i386/autoconf_i386.m.
 4. Add a bringUp:named:withTable: line to +probeBus: in i386/PExpert.m,
    after the buses it depends on.

Drivers on the new bus name it with "Bus Type" in their tables; the kernel
turns that into the class name "<Bus Type>KernBus" and the device
description class "IO<Bus Type>DeviceDescription", so the KernBus subclass
must follow that naming and the description class must exist (the PCI and
PCMCIA ones live in driverkit-3).

Build
-----

rbuild kernel --arch i386 builds this project before kernel-7, as it does
the ppc expert; kernel-7/apk/pkginfo carries makedepends_i386 = drvpexpert.
The Component subprojects do not inherit the Kernel Server's compiler
flags, so each sets -static -DKERNEL -D_KERNEL -DDRIVER_PRIVATE itself, as
the ppc chips/ and families/ subprojects do.

Status
------

Not yet built or boot-tested.  The System.config "Boot Drivers" line no
longer lists EISABus and PCIBus (src/system_config-1/i386/Default.table),
so a kernel without this object linked in has no bus drivers at all.  Not
carried over from drvEISABus: EISABusInspector.nib and the reconstruction
ledgers, which describe the original projects.
